1
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:12,400
ASTRA is one live dashboard for disasters, space safety and navigation over India.

2
00:00:12,500 --> 00:00:16,200
Built entirely on public data: ISRO, NDMA, IMD and the global space catalogue.

3
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:20,300
Official alerts from IMD, CWC and state authorities, mapped within minutes.

4
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:24,300
Select one for the rain, river and wave forecast at that exact spot.

5
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:28,200
Earthquakes, cyclone tracks and radar flood maps that see through cloud.

6
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:31,600
Pick any place in India. Kedarnath, for example.

7
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:34,900
ASTRA rates its flood risk for the next 72 hours, and for a heavy-monsoon storm,

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,800
plus long-term earthquake exposure, all in seconds.

9
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:42,800
Above us, more than 28,000 tracked objects share the sky with India's satellites.

10
00:00:43,100 --> 00:00:46,700
ASTRA screens every close approach predicted this week,

11
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:50,800
each one ranked by miss distance, speed and collision odds.

12
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:54,900
When disaster strikes: which Indian satellites pass overhead in the next 24 hours?

13
00:00:55,100 --> 00:00:59,200
ASTRA lists them, checks each one is safe, and says what each can see.

14
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:03,300
NavIC, India's own navigation constellation, tracked live.

15
00:01:03,600 --> 00:01:07,200
Positioning quality on the ground, computed from the live orbits.

16
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:11,200
When towers fall, an alert is packed into a NavIC message and relayed from orbit.

17
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:14,500
Every number traces back to a public source,

18
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:17,400
and the site labels plainly what is live and what is illustrative.
